import theorem            stdlib/polynomials/remainder
import mundane theorem    stdlib/polynomials/polynomial-value

theorem root-gives-factor
  metamath    facth
  note        The remainder of p on division by x − a is p(a), which is 0, so
              the division leaves no remainder.
  let p be a polynomial                                               (H1)
  let a ∈ ℂ                                                           (H2)
  assume p(a) = 0                                                     (H3)
  then there is a polynomial q with p(x) = (x − a)·q(x) for all x ∈ ℂ

1.  q is a polynomial. p(x) = (x − a)·q(x) + p(a) for all x ∈ ℂ.
    obtain q: thm:remainder, from H1

2.  For all x ∈ ℂ, p(x) = (x − a)·q(x).
    proof
    let x ∈ ℂ                                                         (K1)

    2.1.  p(x) = (x − a)·q(x) + p(a)
          instantiate x := x in line 1

    2.2.  p(x) = (x − a)·q(x) + 0
          substitute p(a) = 0 (H3) into line 2.1

    2.3.  (x − a)·q(x) + 0 = (x − a)·q(x)
          algebra
          requires q(x) ∈ ℂ: mun:polynomial-value p := q, from 1

    2.4.  p(x) = (x − a)·q(x)
          calculation
            p(x) = (x − a)·q(x) + 0     2.2
                 = (x − a)·q(x)         2.3

3.  There is a polynomial q with p(x) = (x − a)·q(x) for all x ∈ ℂ.
    exhibit, from 1, 2
